# The three DO/LOOP forms this crate refuses, on both engines.
#
# **These rows are NOT oracle bytes and the oracle is not the reference for
# them.** It implements all three; this crate declines them, loudly, at
# `rexx-exec: DO is not implemented` and rc 120. `DO COUNTER` needs
# running-count bookkeeping no other form needs, `DO WITH` sends `SUPPLIER` a
# message and nothing here answers messages at all, and a stem `OVER` target is
# Deviation 1 in `phase-4-exclusions.txt` -- its tail order is a balanced tree
# against our hash map. So the expected block below is this crate's own answer,
# and what each row asserts is that the two engines give it identically.
#
# The refusal is one decision (`run.rs`'s `loop_header_plan`, consulted by
# `run_loop_with_header`) rather than one per engine, and these rows are what
# says so. Measured before that was true: the compiled stream reached an
# `unreachable!` for `DO WITH` and an `expect` on an unevaluated value for the
# other two, panicking at rc 101 where the tree-walker refused at rc 120 -- and
# **the whole workspace was green**, because nothing ran a refused loop on the
# compiled stream. A promotion that emits its own refusal instead of asking is
# what these rows are here to catch.

# do with
program
do with index i over 5
  nop
end
----
rc> 120
err> rexx-exec: DO is not implemented

# do counter
program
do counter c i = 1 to 2
  nop
end
----
rc> 120
err> rexx-exec: DO is not implemented

# do over a stem target
program
a. = 1
do qq over a.
  nop
end
----
rc> 120
err> rexx-exec: DO is not implemented
